maria-support: FYI — customer reporting streaming responses arriving out-of-order on their edge deployment. They say tokens jump around (seq numbers look shuffled). Repro on Netlify / Vercel edge runtimes with Node SDK v1.8.0.

maria-support: Minimal log from customer: 
```
[stream] chunk seq=2 payload=\"hello\"\n[stream] chunk seq=1 payload=\" world\"\n[stream] chunk seq=3 payload=\"!\"\n```

maria-support: They read via ReadableStream.getReader()/read() in a route edge handler. Node 18 runtime, SDK 1.8.0, no proxy; sometimes works locally but repros on edge. Customer request id: r-9f3b2c7a.

lee-sdk: thanks, passing to runtime team. quick questions: can you paste the small client read loop (how they consume the stream) and the exact SDK package version (npm ls @redwood/inference or similar)?

maria-support: pasted snippet from customer: 
```
// edge route
const res = await client.stream({ model: 'gpt-small' })
const reader = res.body.getReader()
let done = false
while (!done) {
  const { value, done: d } = await reader.read()
  done = d
  if (value) {
    const s = new TextDecoder().decode(value)
    // append s to buffer
    console.log('[stream] chunk', s)
  }
}
```

lee-sdk: ok. SDK v1.8.0 had a change to transform streams in edge builds. Please try upgrading to v1.9.2 if possible — it includes an edge-stream buffering fix. If upgrade not possible, short workaround: use the body.text() fallback (non-streaming) or re-assemble ordering by parsing the chunk metadata (if present).

tom-runtime: we saw a proxy patch on 2026-02-12 that changed chunk coalescing on some CDN edge nodes. It can cause upstream chunk boundaries to be re-emitted in different micro-batches depending on gzip and HTTP/2 multiplexing. This looks like that class of bug.

rachel-sre: @tom-runtime can you check edge node logs for request id r-9f3b2c7a? We need to confirm if chunks left the origin in order. If origin order is correct, then it's likely CDN coalescing on the way to the runtime.

tom-runtime: pulled logs for r-9f3b2c7a — at origin we have seq=1,2,3 in order. On the edge node there are two write events: first had seq=2 then seq=1 then seq=3. Looks like rebuffer on the edge.

maria-support: customer would like workaround while infra patch rolls out. Any SDK-level mitigation?

lee-sdk: one pragmatic mitigation: avoid relying on chunk boundaries for ordering. Add a small sequence tag in the payload (server/model) so client can reorder safely. Alternatively, set stream mode to "single-chunk" (if your SDK config supports it) so content is emitted as fewer larger chunks. We'll add a note to the SDK changelog.

rachel-sre: infra plan: we have a staged CDN patch to preserve original write boundaries globally. Targeting canary cluster tomorrow morning PST; I'll coordinate with ops.

maria-support: I'll relay to customer: suggest immediate upgrade to SDK v1.9.2 or switch to non-streaming path. Also suggested adding sequence ids as a safety net. Customer thanked us and will try upgrade. :thumbsup:

